1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,714
Coach: Try a different discussion.

2
00:00:01,714 --> 00:00:07,286
Coach: A team is planning a one-page event notice for a community room.

3
00:00:07,286 --> 00:00:13,286
Coach: They want people to notice the event and easily find its time and place.

4
00:00:13,286 --> 00:00:15,429
Coach: No layout has been chosen.

5
00:00:15,779 --> 00:00:18,779
Noah: A large picture could catch people's attention.

6
00:00:18,779 --> 00:00:22,636
Noah: I would use most of the page for it.

7
00:00:22,986 --> 00:00:28,129
Coach: You can recognise the possible benefit without accepting that amount of space.

8
00:00:28,129 --> 00:00:31,129
Coach: If the information becomes difficult to find,

9
00:00:31,129 --> 00:00:33,701
Coach: that would conflict with another aim.

10
00:00:33,701 --> 00:00:38,415
Coach: We have not tested a layout, so keep the concern conditional.

11
00:00:38,765 --> 00:00:41,336
Coach: Privately form one short English reply.

12
00:00:41,336 --> 00:00:46,479
Coach: Make clear which part you question, why it matters to this notice,

13
00:00:46,479 --> 00:00:49,479
Coach: and what the team could examine next.

14
00:00:49,479 --> 00:00:53,765
Coach: Use only the parts needed to make your position clear.

15
00:01:06,115 --> 00:01:08,258
You: The picture could attract attention,

16
00:01:08,258 --> 00:01:14,687
You: but using most of the page may make the time and place harder to find.

17
00:01:14,687 --> 00:01:19,401
You: Could we compare two layouts with those details kept clearly visible?

18
00:01:19,751 --> 00:01:21,894
Noah: Yes, we can compare them.

19
00:01:21,894 --> 00:01:25,322
Noah: I still think the picture should be prominent.

20
00:01:25,672 --> 00:01:31,243
Coach: The reply identifies a specific concern and offers a way to examine it.

21
00:01:31,243 --> 00:01:37,243
Coach: Noah agrees to the comparison in this example, but has not chosen a layout.

22
00:01:37,243 --> 00:01:41,529
Coach: His continuing preference is not a failure of the conversation.

23
00:01:41,879 --> 00:01:44,450
Coach: The comparison still needs to happen.

24
00:01:44,450 --> 00:01:50,451
Coach: We cannot conclude that one layout attracts more people or is easier to read.

25
00:01:50,451 --> 00:01:54,308
Coach: Nor must the team split the page equally just

26
00:01:54,308 --> 00:01:56,451
Coach: because there are different preferences.

27
00:01:56,451 --> 00:01:59,022
Coach: Check the alternatives against the task.

28
00:01:59,372 --> 00:02:02,801
Coach: If you formed a reply, review one feature:

29
00:02:02,801 --> 00:02:07,515
Coach: was the disagreement specific, was the reason connected to the task,

30
00:02:07,515 --> 00:02:10,943
Coach: or did the next step make something examinable?

31
00:02:10,943 --> 00:02:16,086
Coach: If you did not rehearse, choose a feature to try next time.

32
00:02:24,436 --> 00:02:27,007
Coach: You can disagree directly and respectfully.

33
00:02:27,007 --> 00:02:31,293
Coach: You do not have to manufacture agreement, force a compromise,

34
00:02:31,293 --> 00:02:35,150
Coach: or use the same three-part sentence every time.

35
00:02:35,150 --> 00:02:39,007
Coach: Keep the issue clear enough for others to examine.

36
00:02:39,007 --> 00:02:43,293
Coach: You can revisit the examples or try the rehearsal again.
